  "step_2": "The individual considers the monetary outcomes (4000 vs. 3000) and their respective probabilities (20% vs. 25%). They note that both lotteries have no negative outcomes (i.e., minimum is 0). The individual emphasizes the difference in probability: they state that the 5% difference in probability (25% vs. 20%) is significant, and they chose Lottery A because it offered a higher chance of winning (though this is incorrect — A has 20%, B has 25%, so B has higher probability). The individual then reflects that they might have made a mistake, suggesting they now believe B would have been better due to its higher probability. Their justification centers on probability (chance of winning), not variance or risk dispersion. They mention 'pokert' (poker), indicating a possible reference to risk-taking behavior, but no mention of variability, spread, or variance of outcomes is present.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires the individual to compare the variance of the lotteries and prefer the one with higher variance. The individual did not mention variance, spread, or any measure of risk dispersion. Instead, they focused on the probability of winning (25% vs. 20%) and the magnitude of the prize (4000 vs. 3000), concluding that A was chosen because of a higher chance (which is false — B has higher chance). Their reasoning is based on probability and outcome size, not on the statistical variance. Although Lottery A does have higher variance, the individual did not use this as a criterion. Their justification is inconsistent with the variance-based reason and does not reference it at all. Therefore, there is no evidence that the individual used the variance-based decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
